hmmmm, the calm, level-headed signals seem to have been registered
and the niose is increasing appreciably concerning "sink or swim".
let's wind this down ...

fact: pools _are_ hazardous.
question: is it playable?

the best (most persuasive) arguments stuck to the issue and avoided
references to friends, foes or whatever. alternatives were suggested:

0) no change, keep status quo.
1) entering a pool requires confirmation (while retaining status quo).
2) drowning should be circumstantial. you may survive but perhaps
    you'd be better off dead.
3) drowning is completely unrealistic. you should almost always swim
    and never undergo considerable losses.
4) the game needs more water magic (that helps and hurts).

of course, there are many shades of gray in between these points. what
else is there?

personally, i don't think the game should pander to unresponsive
interfaces. that has nothing to do with nethack strategy. i like the
deadly threat of pools. for those that have won the game it is a feature
that commands respect. however, if you've never won then it must seem
like another "arbitrary" way to die. if confirmation is used consider
the fact that currently there is no motivation to enter water and so
the only response will be "no" and the method's utility will only be
to prevent deaths by drowning. that doesn't seem to be in the spirit
of the game. if there were reasons that a player might want to enter
a pool of water then accidently entering a pool of water wouldn't be
as big a mistake as it is now. i can entertain notions of problematic,
circumstantially advantageous/disadvantageous pools. other features
have grown along these lines. why not pools? i would sincerely hope
this feature is not eliminated merely because it extremely hazardous.
